research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-93-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-94-The first local definition must state \(P\to M\) as a smooth right principal \(G\)-bundle and distinguish the structure group \(G\), Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), gauge group \(\mathcal G(P)\), and local gauge function. A gauge transformation of a nontrivial bundle is an equivariant bundle automorphism covering \(\mathrm{id}_M\); it is not globally an arbitrary map \(M\to G\). Such maps describe the trivialized/local expression in a chosen chart.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-95-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-96-Given the existing horizontal distribution \(H\), define the fundamental vertical vector at \(p\) by \(Z_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_0p\exp(tZ)\), with the right-action convention. Prove that \(Z\mapsto Z_P(p)\) is the required fibrewise isomorphism, then define the connection form \(\omega\) by applying its inverse to the vertical projection of \(X\in T_pP\). Prove \(\omega(Z_P)=Z\), \(R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega\), and \(\ker\omega=H\). Define \(\Omega=d\omega+\tfrac12[\omega\wedge\omega]\), prove horizontality/equivariance, and compare it to the existing vertical curvature \(K_H\); this pins down the sign rather than silently replacing its convention.
