items/lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility.md:57:1.1 Average a Riemannian metric over normalized Haar measure on $T$. At $p\in F$, the exponential map of this invariant metric is $T$-equivariant and is injective on a sufficiently small ball in $T_pM$. Thus, for $v$ in that ball, $\exp_p(v)$ is fixed by every $t\in T$ exactly when $v$ is fixed by every linear map $d_pt$; the fixed set near $p$ is the exponential image of $(T_pM)^T$. Therefore $F$ is locally a smooth submanifold and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$. [given, A1, construct]
items/lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities.md:53:1.1 Average any Riemannian metric on $M$ over normalized Haar measure on $T$ to obtain a $T$-invariant metric. The normal exponential map is $T$-equivariant; its derivative is invertible along the zero section, so the inverse function theorem and compactness of $N$ give a $T$-invariant tubular neighborhood identified with $\nu$. Since $T$ fixes $N$ pointwise, $ET\times_T N\cong BT\times N$, and the Borel normal bundle $ET\times_T\nu$ is an oriented rank-$c$ vector bundle over the CW space $BT\times N$. [given, A1, F1, construct]
