items/def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data.md:49:Assume Countable Choice. Let $\Gamma=(V,E,s,t,\mathcal P)$ be a finite oriented graph with nonempty finite vertex set $V$, nonempty finite link set $E$, endpoint maps $s,t:E\to V$, and a finite list $\mathcal P$ of oriented simple cycles. Parallel links and self-loops are allowed. An oriented simple cycle $p\in\mathcal P$ is a finite closed path using each link at most once; write $\sigma_{p,e}\in\{-1,0,1\}$ for its signed use of link $e$, so its signed incidence at every vertex is zero. Fix enumerations $E=(\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_N)$ and $V=(v_1,\ldots,v_M)$, where $N=|E|$ and $M=|V|$. Write $\mathbb T_1=\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$ and $\mathbb T_{2\pi}=\mathbb R/(2\pi\mathbb Z)$, and set $\mathbb T=\mathbb T_{2\pi}$ with normalized Haar measure $d\theta/(2\pi)$ and product measure $m_E$ on $\mathbb T^E$.
items/def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data.md:53:is a well-defined measure-preserving group isomorphism for the normalized product Haar measures. The fixed enumeration of $E$ identifies $\mathbb T_1^E$ with $\mathbb T_1^N$; pulling back the Fourier characters $x\mapsto\exp(2\pi i\sum_{e\in E}n_ex_e)$ along $\Phi_E$ gives $e_{\mathbf n}(\theta)=\exp(i\sum_{e\in E}n_e\theta_e)$. Thus the Fourier basis theorem [[thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus]], whose torus is $\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$ and whose characters are $\exp(2\pi i kx)$, gives an orthonormal basis $\{e_{\mathbf n}:\mathbf n\in\mathbb Z^E\}$ of $L^2(\mathbb T^E)$. The second-variable-linear $L^2$ pairing used below is the conjugate of the first-variable-linear pairing in that theorem, so orthonormality and completeness are unchanged.
items/def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data.md:80:and let $G_x$ be its maximal diagonal operator, with domain $D(G_x)=\{\psi:\sum_{\mathbf n,\mathbf m}|\gamma_x(\mathbf n,\mathbf m)|^2|\psi_{\mathbf n,\mathbf m}|^2<\infty\}$. On the common link-electric domain this is the finite sum $\sum_{s(e)=x}E_e-\sum_{t(e)=x}E_e-(n_x-b_xI)$. These are the Gauss operators; the operator constraint defines $\mathcal H_{\rm phys}:=\bigcap_{x\in V}\ker G_x$. For $\alpha\in\mathbb T^V$ define the diagonal multiplier $U(\alpha)=\exp(i\sum_x\alpha_xG_x)$; the next lemma proves that these operators form a strongly continuous representation and that their normalized Haar average is the orthogonal projection onto $\mathcal H_{\rm phys}$.
