@@ -0,0 +1,97 @@
+---
+id: lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined
+kind: lemma
+title: Finite SU(2) Wilson integrals and transfer-sector well-definedness
+status: draft
+origin: session
+dependency_level: 1
+domain: mathematics
+library: physics
+category: advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry
+deps:
+  - def-axiom-of-choice
+  - cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group
+  - def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data
+  - lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group
+dependency_roles:
+  def-axiom-of-choice: mathematical-premise
+  cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group: mathematical-premise
+  def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data: mathematical-premise
+  lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group: mathematical-premise
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Michael Creutz, Quarks, Gluons and Lattices, Chapters 7 and 8"
+      url: "https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/8BE1322C90332E2092E3945035525382/9781009290388c7_34-38.pdf/lattice_gauge_theory.pdf"
+    - title: "David Tong, Gauge Theory, Chapter 4, Lattice Gauge Theory"
+      url: "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge4.pdf"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let \(N_s,N_t\geq3\) be finite integers and let \(a>0\), \(\beta>0\). Use the finite periodic Euclidean lattice \(\Lambda=(\mathbb Z/N_s\mathbb Z)^3\times(\mathbb Z/N_t\mathbb Z)\), with positive spatial edges \((x,i,t)\) and temporal edges \((x,t)\). On every positive edge put an independent matrix in the compact Hausdorff group
+
+$$SU(2)=\{U\in M_2(\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\ \det U=1\},$$
+
+and assign its inverse to reverse traversal. A vertex function \(\Omega:\Lambda\to SU(2)\) acts on an oriented link \(U_{vw}\) by \(U_{vw}\mapsto\Omega_vU_{vw}\Omega_w^{-1}\). For each oriented plaquette \(p\), let \(U_p\) be the ordered product of its four boundary links, using inverse matrices for reverse traversals. Put
+
+$$S_\Lambda(U)=\beta\sum_{p\in\mathcal P_\Lambda}\left(1-\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2U_p\right).$$
+
+Let \(dU\) be the unique normalized left Haar probability measure on \(SU(2)\), which is also right invariant, and let \(d\mu_\Lambda\) be its finite product over all positive links. Define
+
+$$Z_\Lambda=\int e^{-S_\Lambda(U)}\,d\mu_\Lambda(U).$$
+
+For every bounded measurable function \(O\) on the finite link configuration space, the integral \(\int Oe^{-S_\Lambda}\,d\mu_\Lambda\) is finite; moreover \(0<Z_\Lambda\leq1\), so its normalized expectation is defined.
+
+For a spatial slice \(u=(U_{x,i})\), define the spatial plaquette action \(S_s(u)\) by restricting the displayed action to spatial plaquettes. For two adjacent slices \(u',u\) and temporal links \(\eta_x\), define \(S_t(u',u;\eta)\) by summing the same plaquette term over \(\eta_xU'_{x,i}\eta_{x+\hat i}^{-1}U_{x,i}^{-1}\). Let \(d\mu_s,d\mu_V\) be the corresponding finite product Haar probabilities, let \(\mathcal H_{\rm kin}=L^2(SU(2)^{E_s},d\mu_s)\), and let \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\) be the subspace fixed by the spatial vertex-gauge action. Define
+
+$$K(u',u)=e^{-\frac12S_s(u')-\frac12S_s(u)}\int e^{-S_t(u',u;\eta)}\,d\mu_V(\eta),\qquad (T\psi)(u')=\int K(u',u)\psi(u)\,d\mu_s(u).$$
+
+Then \(T\) is a bounded operator on \(\mathcal H_{\rm kin}\) with \(\|T\|\leq1\), maps \(\mathcal H_{\rm kin}\) into \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\), and \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\) contains the nonzero constant function. The cyclic product of \(N_t\) kernels integrates to \(Z_\Lambda\); insertion of any bounded slice observable gives the corresponding finite-volume unnormalized expectation. No self-adjointness, positivity as a quadratic form, Hamiltonian limit, or continuum statement is asserted.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[[def-axiom-of-choice|Axiom of Choice used for normalized Haar probability]]
+
+[[cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group|Normalized Haar probability on a compact Hausdorff group]]
+
+[[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group|Compact Hausdorff structure of SU(2)]]
+
+[[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data|Planned finite compact-G lattice-data interface]]
+
+[Creutz, *Quarks, Gluons and Lattices*, Chapter 7, pp. 34–38](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/8BE1322C90332E2092E3945035525382/9781009290388c7_34-38.pdf/lattice_gauge_theory.pdf)
+
+[Creutz, *Quarks, Gluons and Lattices*, Chapter 8, pp. 39–43](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D249BE42514DA3218D7060836C8438ED/9781009290388c8_39-50.pdf/group_integration.pdf)
+
+[Tong, *Gauge Theory*, Chapter 4, §§4.2.1–4.2.3, pp. 206–215](https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge4.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Full AC; finite \(N_s,N_t\geq3\), \(a,\beta>0\); the finite oriented lattice and compact-group link-data convention stated above.
+
+[A1] Full AC, written as the choice-function axiom, is used only through normalized Haar existence. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
+
+[F1] [[cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group]]: under AC, a compact Hausdorff group has a unique normalized left Haar probability, also right invariant.
+
+[F2] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group]]: the displayed matrix group is compact Hausdorff and non-Abelian; its compactness proof uses the choice-free Heine–Borel theorem.
+
+[F3] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]] is the planned, currently absent mathematical interface for finite oriented links, vertex gauge action, plaquette products, compact-group edge variables, and normalized product Haar data. All claims below are conditional on that exact planned interface until its carrier is available and inspected.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 For \(V\in SU(2)\), write its first column as \((\alpha,\gamma)\). Its second unit column orthogonal to the first is \(\lambda(-\overline{\gamma},\overline{\alpha})\) for \(|\lambda|=1\); determinant one gives \(\lambda(|\alpha|^2+|\gamma|^2)=\lambda=1\). Hence \(V=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\-\overline{\beta}&\overline{\alpha}\end{pmatrix}\) with \(|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1\), so \(\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V=\operatorname{Re}\alpha\in[-1,1]\). [given, F2, algebra]
+
+1.2 Each plaquette term therefore lies in \([0,2]\). Since \(\mathcal P_\Lambda\) is finite, \(0\leq S_\Lambda\leq2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|\), and consequently \(e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}\leq e^{-S_\Lambda}\leq1\). [given, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+1.3 The finite product Haar measure is a probability by [F1] and the finite-product definition in [F3]. Integrating the two bounds in step 1.2 gives \(e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}\leq Z_\Lambda\leq1\). For bounded \(O\), \(\left|\int Oe^{-S_\Lambda}\,d\mu_\Lambda\right|\leq\|O\|_\infty Z_\Lambda<\infty\); division by the strictly positive lower bound for \(Z_\Lambda\) defines its normalized expectation. [given, A1, F1, F3, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.1 The actions \(S_s,S_t\) are sums of the same nonnegative plaquette terms. Thus \(0<K(u',u)\leq1\). For each fixed \(u'\), Cauchy–Schwarz with measure \(K(u',u)d\mu_s(u)\) yields \(|T\psi(u')|^2\leq\int K(u',u)d\mu_s(u)\int K(u',u)|\psi(u)|^2d\mu_s(u)\leq\|\psi\|_2^2\). Integrating over \(u'\) against the probability \(d\mu_s\) gives \(\|T\psi\|_2\leq\|\psi\|_2\). [given, F1, F3, algebra]
+
+3.1 Under independent spatial gauge transformations \(h,h'\) of the incoming and outgoing slices, change variables \(\eta_x\mapsto h_x\eta_x(h'_x)^{-1}\). Each temporal plaquette product is conjugated by \(h_x\), and each spatial plaquette is conjugated at its base vertex, so trace invariance preserves \(S_t,S_s\); [F1] preserves \(d\mu_V\). Hence \(K(h'\cdot u',h\cdot u)=K(u',u)\). Integrating against any \(\psi\) shows \(T\psi\) is fixed by the outgoing gauge group, so \(T\mathcal H_{\rm kin}\subseteq\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\). The constant function has norm one because \(d\mu_s\) is a probability and is fixed by every gauge transformation, so it is a nonzero physical vector. [given, F1, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+4.1 In the cyclic product of kernels, each time slice contributes one half of \(S_s(u_t)\) from the incoming factor and one half from the outgoing factor, summing to the full spatial action. Each temporal-link integral contributes exactly \(e^{-S_t(u_{t+1},u_t;\eta_t)}\). Therefore the product integrand is the full \(e^{-S_\Lambda}\); finite-product Tonelli integration gives the stated cyclic-kernel integral \(Z_\Lambda\), and insertion of bounded \(O\) gives its unnormalized expectation. [given, F3, step 1.3, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
