+
+## Example
+
+Fix finite integers \(N_s,N_t\geq3\), spacing \(a>0\), and \(g_0>0\) in the adopted pure SU(2) Euclidean Wilson regulator. On one spatial slice, let \(C\) be any finite closed oriented lattice path and let \(U_C\) be the ordered product of its link matrices, using \(U_\ell^{-1}\) whenever the path traverses \(\ell\) backwards. Define the normalized loop function \(W_C(u)=\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2(U_C)\) and its multiplication operator \(M_{W_C}\) on the slice space. Then \(W_C\) is gauge invariant and \(|W_C|\leq1\); the finite-volume expectation \(Z_\Lambda^{-1}\int W_Ce^{-S_\Lambda}\,d\mu_\Lambda\) is well-defined. The one-step transfer operator \(T\) is bounded and maps the group-invariant Gauss sector \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\) into itself, which contains the nonzero constant wavefunction. Iterating its kernel reproduces the finite periodic Euclidean lattice integral. These are exact finite-regulator statements; they establish no continuum area law or Lorentzian energy spectrum.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Creutz, *Quarks, Gluons and Lattices*, Chapter 7, pp. 34–38](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/8BE1322C90332E2092E3945035525382/9781009290388c7_34-38.pdf/lattice_gauge_theory.pdf)
+
+[Creutz, *Quarks, Gluons and Lattices*, Chapter 8, pp. 39–43](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D249BE42514DA3218D7060836C8438ED/9781009290388c8_39-50.pdf/group_integration.pdf)
+
+[Creutz, *Quarks, Gluons and Lattices*, Chapter 15, pp. 101–107](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/1B676F70F134E1133DD3D1E56C42C0D3/9781009290388c15_101-107.pdf/the-hamiltonian-approach.pdf)
+
+[Tong, *Gauge Theory*, Chapter 4, §§4.2.1–4.2.3, pp. 206–215](https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge4.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The exact finite SU(2) lattice, Haar product measure, action, spatial gauge action, physical slice sector, and transfer kernel of [[post-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-regulator]], with \(N_s,N_t\geq3\), \(a>0\), \(g_0>0\), and natural units \(\hbar=c=1\).
+
+[A1] The regulator is adopted in [[post-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-regulator]]; all deductions below are conditional on that Euclidean finite-volume model.
+
+[A2] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: every family of nonempty sets has a choice function; here it is assumed only for normalized Haar-probability existence.
+
+[F1] [[cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group]]: under full AC, a compact Hausdorff group has a unique normalized left Haar probability, which is right invariant.
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 For a closed path \(C=(x_0,\ell_1,x_1,\ldots,\ell_m,x_m)\) with \(x_m=x_0\), each oriented link factor transforms as \(\Omega_{x_{k-1}}U_{\ell_k}^{\epsilon_k}\Omega_{x_k}^{-1}\), where \(\epsilon_k=1\) for forward traversal and \(-1\) for reverse traversal. Adjacent gauge factors cancel in the ordered product, so \(U_C\mapsto\Omega_{x_0}U_C\Omega_{x_0}^{-1}\); cyclicity of trace gives \(W_C(\Omega\cdot u)=W_C(u)\). For \(V\in SU(2)\), write its first column as \((\alpha,\gamma)\). Its second unit column orthogonal to the first is \(\lambda(-\overline{\gamma},\overline{\alpha})\) for some \(|\lambda|=1\); the determinant of the two columns is \(\lambda(|\alpha|^2+|\gamma|^2)=\lambda\), so determinant one forces \(\lambda=1\). Thus \(V=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\-\overline{\beta}&\overline{\alpha}\end{pmatrix}\) with \(|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1\), whence \(\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V=\operatorname{Re}\alpha\in[-1,1]\). Therefore \(M_{W_C}\) is a bounded operator of norm at most one and preserves \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\). [given, A1, algebra]
+
+1.2 The same vertex transformations conjugate every spatial or temporal plaquette product at its base vertex, so every real trace term in \(S_\Lambda\) is unchanged. Each link transformation is left and right multiplication by fixed group elements; [F1] gives the invariance needed for the finite product measure. Every plaquette term is nonnegative by the trace bound in step 1.1, hence \(0<e^{-S_\Lambda}\leq1\). A finite product of normalized probability measures has total mass one, so \(Z_\Lambda\leq1\). There are finitely many plaquettes and each term in \(S_\Lambda\) is at most \(2\beta\), hence \(S_\Lambda\leq2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|\) and \(Z_\Lambda\geq e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}>0\). Therefore the normalized loop expectation exists and has absolute value at most one. [given, A1, A2, F1, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 Under independent gauge transformations \(h\) and \(h'\) of the incoming and outgoing slices, change the temporal-link integration variables by \(\eta_x\mapsto h_x\eta_x(h'_x)^{-1}\). Each temporal plaquette product in \(S_t(u',u;\eta)\) is then conjugated by \(h_x\); its trace is unchanged, while [F1] preserves the product Haar measure \(d\mu_V\). The spatial half-actions in \(K\) are gauge invariant by step 1.2. Thus \(K(h'\cdot u',h\cdot u)=K(u',u)\). In particular, for any slice wavefunction \(\psi\), \(T\psi\) is invariant under the outgoing vertex group, so \(T\) maps \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\) into itself. The constant function \(1\) has squared norm \(\int 1\,d\mu_s=1\), is fixed by every vertex transformation, and is therefore a nonzero member of \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\). [given, A1, A2, F1, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.2 The actions \(S_s,S_t\) are nonnegative, and all Haar measures in \(K\) are probability measures; hence \(0<K(u',u)\leq1\). For \(\psi\in\mathcal H_{\rm kin}\), Cauchy–Schwarz with the measure \(K(u',u)d\mu_s(u)\) gives \(|(T\psi)(u')|^2\leq\int K(u',u)d\mu_s(u)\int K(u',u)|\psi(u)|^2d\mu_s(u)\leq\|\psi\|_2^2\); integrating in \(u'\) yields \(\|T\psi\|_2\leq\|\psi\|_2\). Finally, each product of adjacent kernels contributes one half of the spatial action from each of its two slices and one complete temporal-spatial action from its temporal links. In the cyclic product, the two halves at each slice sum to its full spatial action; Tonelli's theorem permits rearranging the finite integrals of the nonnegative integrand, so the cyclic kernel integral is exactly \(Z_\Lambda\), and inserting \(W_C\) on one slice gives its stated normalized expectation. [given, A1, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
