+---
+id: lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group
+kind: lemma
+title: Compact Hausdorffness and non-Abelianness of SU(2)
+status: draft
+origin: session
+dependency_level: 0
+domain: mathematics
+library: physics
+category: advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry
+deps:
+  - thm-heine-borel-rn
+dependency_roles:
+  thm-heine-borel-rn: mathematical-premise
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Michael Creutz, Quarks, Gluons and Lattices, Chapter 8, Group integration"
+      url: "https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D249BE42514DA3218D7060836C8438ED/9781009290388c8_39-50.pdf/group_integration.pdf"
+    - title: "David Tong, Gauge Theory, Chapter 4, Lattice Gauge Theory"
+      url: "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge4.pdf"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let
+
+$$SU(2):=\{U\in M_2(\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\ \det U=1\}$$
+
+with matrix multiplication and the topology induced by the eight real and imaginary matrix-entry coordinates. Then \(SU(2)\) is a compact Hausdorff non-Abelian topological group.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Creutz, *Quarks, Gluons and Lattices*, Chapter 8, pp. 39–43](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D249BE42514DA3218D7060836C8438ED/9781009290388c8_39-50.pdf/group_integration.pdf)
+
+[Tong, *Gauge Theory*, Chapter 4, §4.2.2, pp. 210–212](https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge4.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The matrix set and topology stated above, and the Euclidean metric on its eight real coordinates.
+
+[F1] [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]: in finite-dimensional Euclidean space, every closed and bounded subset is compact. The theorem's proof uses no choice principle.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 If \(U,V\in SU(2)\), then \((UV)^*(UV)=V^*U^*UV=I_2\) and \(\det(UV)=\det U\det V=1\). The identity belongs to \(SU(2)\), and \(U^{-1}=U^*\) is unitary with determinant \(\overline{\det U}=1\). Thus the set is a group under matrix multiplication, with the inherited continuous multiplication and inversion. [given, algebra]
+
+1.2 In real coordinates the equations \(U^*U=I_2\) and \(\det U=1\) are continuous equations, so \(SU(2)\) is closed. Unitarity makes each column a unit vector, hence every real and imaginary entry lies in \([-1,1]\); the set is bounded in \(\mathbb R^8\). Apply [F1] to conclude compactness. The inherited Euclidean metric makes this compact subspace Hausdorff: distinct \(U,V\) have disjoint open balls of radius \(\|U-V\|/3\). [given, F1, algebra]
+
+2.1 The matrices \(A=\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\0&-i\end{pmatrix}\) and \(B=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}\) are unitary with determinant one, while \(AB=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}\) and \(BA=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\-i&0\end{pmatrix}\) differ. Therefore \(SU(2)\) is non-Abelian. [given, algebra] ∎
