[F6] [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]: for sigma-finite factor measures, the product measure exists and has the rectangle formula. Applying this two-factor theorem successively to the finite list of Haar probability factors gives the finite product measure; at each stage its total mass is one by the rectangle formula.

## Proof

1.1 For \(V\in SU(2)\), write its first column as \((\alpha,\gamma)\). Its second unit column orthogonal to the first is \(\lambda(-\overline{\gamma},\overline{\alpha})\) for \(|\lambda|=1\); determinant one gives \(\lambda(|\alpha|^2+|\gamma|^2)=\lambda=1\). Hence \(V=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\-\overline{\beta}&\overline{\alpha}\end{pmatrix}\) with \(|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1\), so \(\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V=\operatorname{Re}\alpha\in[-1,1]\). [given, algebra]

1.2 Each plaquette term therefore lies in \([0,2]\). Since \(\mathcal P_\Lambda\) is finite, \(0\leq S_\Lambda\leq2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|\), and consequently \(e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}\leq e^{-S_\Lambda}\leq1\). [given, step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 The compact Hausdorff group in [F2] has its normalized Haar probability by [F1]. Apply the two-factor product-measure theorem [F6] successively along the finite ordered link set; the rectangle formula at each stage gives total mass one, and [F3] identifies this iterated product with \(d\mu_\Lambda\). Integrating the bounds in step 1.2 gives \(e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}\leq Z_\Lambda\leq1\). For bounded \(O\), \(\left|\int Oe^{-S_\Lambda}\,d\mu_\Lambda\right|\leq\|O\|_\infty Z_\Lambda<\infty\); division by the positive lower bound for \(Z_\Lambda\) defines its normalized expectation. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, F6, step 1.2, algebra]

2.1 The actions \(S_s,S_t\) are sums of nonnegative plaquette terms by step 1.1, so \(0\leq K(u',u)\leq1\). Matrix multiplication, inversion on \(SU(2)\), and trace are continuous, so the temporal-action integrand is nonnegative and product-measurable. The slice and vertex product Haar measures are probabilities by [F1,F6], hence sigma-finite; Tonelli [F4] makes the \(\eta\)-integral \(K\) measurable in \((u',u)\). Each slice Haar product is a probability by [F6], so \(\int|K|^2\,d(\mu_s\times\mu_s)\leq1\). [given, F1, F3, F4, F6, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 The slice measures are finite and hence sigma-finite. Under full AC [A1], apply [F5] to the square-integrable kernel \(K\) from step 2.1 to obtain a well-defined bounded integral operator \(T\) on the complex slice \(L^2\) spaces, with \(\|T\|\leq\|K\|_2\leq1\). [given, A1, F3, F5, step 2.1]

3.1 Each spatial-link transformation is a left and right translation, so it preserves its Haar factor by [F1]; the rectangle formula and uniqueness in [F6] then show the product slice measure \(d\mu_s\) is preserved. Under independent gauge transformations \(h,h'\) of the incoming and outgoing slices, change temporal-link variables by \(\eta_x\mapsto h_x\eta_x(h'_x)^{-1}\). Each such coordinate map also preserves Haar factors by [F1], and thus preserves \(d\mu_V\) by [F6]. Substitution in \(Q_{x,i}=\eta_xU'_{x,i}\eta_{x+\hat i}^{-1}U_{x,i}^{-1}\) cancels the neighboring \(h'\) matrices and gives \(Q_{x,i}\mapsto h_xQ_{x,i}h_x^{-1}\); each spatial plaquette is likewise conjugated at its base vertex. Trace invariance therefore preserves \(S_t,S_s\), and the temporal integration gives \(K(h'\cdot u',h\cdot u)=K(u',u)\) for every pair \((h',h)\). In particular, setting \(h=1\) gives \(K(h'\cdot u',u)=K(u',u)\). The integral representation in [F5], applied almost everywhere, then gives \((T\psi)(h'\cdot u')=(T\psi)(u')\) almost everywhere for each fixed \(h'\), so \(T\psi\in\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\). The constant function has norm one because \(d\mu_s\) is a probability and is fixed by every gauge transformation, so it is a nonzero physical vector. [given, A1, F1, F3, F5, F6, step 2.2, algebra]

3.2 Each \(\mathcal U(h)\) is unitary by measure preservation from step 3.1; the Hilbert structure follows from [F5] under full AC [A1]. Its fixed space is the kernel of the bounded operator \(\mathcal U(h)-I\), hence is closed; the intersection over all \(h\) is \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\), so \(\mathcal H_{\rm phys}\) is closed. [given, A1, F5, step 3.1, algebra]

4.1 In the cyclic product of kernels, each time slice contributes one half of \(S_s(u_t)\) from the incoming factor and one half from the outgoing factor, summing to the full spatial action. Each temporal-link integral contributes exactly \(e^{-S_t(u_{t+1},u_t;\eta_t)}\). Therefore the product integrand before temporal-link integration is the full \(e^{-S_\Lambda}\); finite-product Tonelli [F4] gives the cyclic-kernel integral \(Z_\Lambda\). For a bounded slice observable \(O(u_0)\), the absolute value of its inserted integrand is bounded by \(\|O\|_\infty e^{-S_\Lambda}\leq\|O\|_\infty\) on the finite product probability space [F6], so it is integrable. Applying [F4] to the positive and negative parts of its real and imaginary components justifies exchanging the integrations; the result is the corresponding unnormalized finite-volume expectation. [given, F3, F4, F6, step 1.2, step 1.3, algebra] ∎

