items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:62:[A1] The regulator is adopted in [[post-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-regulator]]; all deductions below are conditional on that Euclidean finite-volume model.
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:64:[F1] [[lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined]]: conditionally on its finite-lattice data input, bounded Wilson-loop expectations exist, the transfer operator is a contraction mapping into the closed group-invariant sector, the constant state is nonzero, and cyclic transfer kernels reconstruct the finite Euclidean integral.
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:66:[A2] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: every family of nonempty sets has a choice function; full AC is used for normalized Haar probability and the $L^2$ transfer-operator framework.
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:68:[F2] [[cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group]]: under full AC, a compact Hausdorff group has a unique normalized left Haar probability that is right invariant.
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:70:[F3] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group]]: every SU(2) element has matrix form $\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\-\overline{\beta}&\overline{\alpha}\end{pmatrix}$ with $|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1$, so $\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2 U\in[-1,1]$.
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:74:1.1 When every link is $I_2$, every plaquette product is $I_2$. Each action term is $\beta(1-\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2 I_2)=0$, so $S_\Lambda=0$; the ordered product around $C$ is $I_2$, hence $W_C=1$. This evaluates the observable on one configuration only, not its probability or expectation. [given, A1, algebra]
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:76:1.2 For a closed path $C=(x_0,\ell_1,x_1,\ldots,\ell_m,x_m)$ with $x_m=x_0$, each oriented factor transforms by the gauge matrix at its start and the inverse gauge matrix at its end. Adjacent factors cancel, leaving $U_C\mapsto\Omega_{x_0}U_C\Omega_{x_0}^{-1}$; cyclicity of trace proves gauge invariance. By [F3], for $V\in SU(2)$ one has $\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V=\operatorname{Re}\alpha\in[-1,1]$. Thus $W_C$ is bounded by one in absolute value. [given, A1, F3, algebra]
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:78:1.3 The choices $N_s=N_t=3$, $a>0$, and $g_0>0$ satisfy the finite-lattice hypotheses of [F1]. Applying [F1] gives a finite positive partition function, a defined normalized expectation for this bounded $W_C$, a bounded transfer operator preserving the Gauss sector, and a nonzero constant vector in that sector. [given, A1, F1, A2, F2]
items/ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector.md:80:2.1 The same application of [F1] gives that the cyclic product of the one-step kernels integrates to the full periodic Euclidean Wilson weight, and that inserting $W_C$ on the chosen slice yields its stated finite-volume expectation. This is a fixed-lattice calculation; it contains no continuum or Lorentzian spectral assertion. [given, A1, F1, step 1.3] ∎
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:62:[A1] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ means every sequence of nonempty sets has an indexed choice function. [[def-countable-choice]]
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:64:[F1] [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]: in finite-dimensional Euclidean space, every closed and bounded subset is compact. The theorem's proof uses no choice principle.
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:66:[F2] [[ex-unitary-and-special-unitary-lie-groups]]: the displayed matrix group $SU(2)$ has its standard finite-dimensional real Lie-group structure as a smooth embedded subgroup of $U(2)$; the matrix inclusion is smooth.
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:68:[F3] [[def-lie-group]]: a finite-dimensional real Lie group is a group with a finite-dimensional real smooth-manifold structure and smooth multiplication and inversion.
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:72:1.1 If $U,V\in SU(2)$, then $(UV)^*(UV)=V^*U^*UV=I_2$ and $\det(UV)=\det U\det V=1$. The identity belongs to $SU(2)$, and $U^{-1}=U^*$ is unitary with determinant $\overline{\det U}=1$. Thus the set is a group. Its standard smooth Lie-group structure and smooth matrix inclusion into $U(2)$ are supplied by [F2] under the definition [F3]. The map $\rho_2$ is a group homomorphism because it is the matrix inclusion, and it is unitary because $U^*U=I_2$. [given, A1, F2, F3, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:74:1.2 In real coordinates the equations $U^*U=I_2$ and $\det U=1$ are continuous equations, so $SU(2)$ is closed. Unitarity makes each column a unit vector, hence every real and imaginary entry lies in $[-1,1]$; the set is bounded in $\mathbb R^8$. Apply [F1] to conclude compactness. The inherited Euclidean metric makes this subspace Hausdorff: for distinct $U,V$, the open balls of radius $\|U-V\|/3$ are disjoint, since a point in both would give $\|U-V\|<2\|U-V\|/3$ by the triangle inequality. [given, F1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:76:1.3 The matrices $A=\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\0&-i\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$ are unitary with determinant one, while $AB=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}$ and $BA=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\-i&0\end{pmatrix}$ differ. Therefore $SU(2)$ is non-Abelian. [given, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group.md:78:2.1 For $V\in SU(2)$, write its first column as $(\alpha,\gamma)$. Its second unit column orthogonal to the first is $\lambda(-\overline{\gamma},\overline{\alpha})$ for $|\lambda|=1$; determinant one gives $\lambda(|\alpha|^2+|\gamma|^2)=\lambda=1$. Hence $V=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\-\overline{\beta}&\overline{\alpha}\end{pmatrix}$ with $|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1$, and $\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V=\operatorname{Re}\alpha\in[-1,1]$. Also $\operatorname{Tr}_2(V^{-1})=\operatorname{Tr}_2(V^*)=\overline{\operatorname{Tr}_2V}=\operatorname{Tr}_2V$, while cyclicity gives $\operatorname{Tr}_2(gVg^{-1})=\operatorname{Tr}_2V$. Thus the normalized trace is real, bounded, conjugation invariant, and inverse invariant. [given, algebra] ∎
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:90:[A1] Full AC, written as the choice-function axiom, is used for normalized Haar existence and the Hilbert-space kernel-operator supplier. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:92:[F1] [[cor-normalized-haar-probability-on-a-compact-group]]: under full AC, a compact Hausdorff group has a unique normalized left Haar probability, also right invariant.
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:94:[F2] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group]]: the displayed matrix group is a compact Hausdorff non-Abelian finite-dimensional real Lie group; its defining matrix representation is smooth and unitary, and $\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V\in[-1,1]$.
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:96:[F3] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]]: the actual finite-lattice interface takes a finite oriented two-complex and a supplied compact noncommutative finite-dimensional Lie group with smooth unitary representation; it assigns group elements to positively oriented edges, uses inverses on reversed edges, defines the vertex gauge action and ordered face holonomies, chooses a normalized bi-invariant Borel probability and its finite product, and uses continuous conjugation- and inverse-invariant face potentials.
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:98:[F4] [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] makes parameter integrals of nonnegative measurable product functions measurable and permits the iterated finite-lattice integrations.
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:100:[F5] [[thm-l-two-kernels-give-hilbert-schmidt-operators]]: under full AC and sigma-finite factor measures, a square-integrable kernel defines a bounded operator between the complex $L^2$ spaces with operator norm at most the kernel $L^2$ norm; those $L^2$ spaces have the required Hilbert structure.
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:102:[F6] [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]: for sigma-finite factor measures, the product measure exists and has the rectangle formula. Applying this two-factor theorem successively to the finite list of Haar probability factors gives the finite product measure; at each stage its total mass is one by the rectangle formula.
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:106:1.1 For $V\in SU(2)$, the parametrization in [F2] gives $\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V\in[-1,1]$ and a real trace. Thus $\Phi(V)=\beta(1-\tfrac12\operatorname{ReTr}_2V)$ is continuous. Cyclicity of trace gives $\operatorname{Tr}_2(gVg^{-1})=\operatorname{Tr}_2V$; unitarity gives $\operatorname{Tr}_2(V^{-1})=\overline{\operatorname{Tr}_2V}=\operatorname{Tr}_2V$. Hence each plaquette potential is conjugation- and inverse-invariant as required by [F3]. [given, F2, F3, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:108:2.1 Each plaquette term therefore lies in $[0,2]$. Since $\mathcal P_\Lambda$ is finite, $0\leq S_\Lambda\leq2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|$, and consequently $e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}\leq e^{-S_\Lambda}\leq1$. [given, step 1.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:110:2.2 The actions $S_s,S_t$ are sums of nonnegative plaquette terms by step 1.1, so $0\leq K(u',u)\leq1$. Matrix multiplication, inversion on $SU(2)$, and trace are continuous, so the temporal-action integrand is nonnegative and product-measurable. The slice and vertex product Haar measures are probabilities by [F1,F6], hence sigma-finite; Tonelli [F4] makes the $\eta$-integral $K$ measurable in $(u',u)$. Each slice Haar product is a probability by [F6], so $\int|K|^2\,d(\mu_s\times\mu_s)\leq1$. [given, F1, F3, F4, F6, step 1.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:112:3.1 The compact Hausdorff group in [F2] has its normalized Haar probability by [F1]. Apply the two-factor product-measure theorem [F6] successively along the finite ordered link set; the rectangle formula at each stage gives total mass one, and [F3] identifies this iterated product with $d\mu_\Lambda$. Integrating the bounds in step 2.1 gives $e^{-2\beta|\mathcal P_\Lambda|}\leq Z_\Lambda\leq1$. For bounded $O$, $\left|\int Oe^{-S_\Lambda}\,d\mu_\Lambda\right|\leq\|O\|_\infty Z_\Lambda<\infty$; division by the positive lower bound for $Z_\Lambda$ defines its normalized expectation. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, F6, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:114:3.2 The slice measures are finite and hence sigma-finite. Under full AC [A1], apply [F5] to the square-integrable kernel $K$ from step 2.2 to obtain a well-defined bounded integral operator $T$ on the complex slice $L^2$ spaces, with $\|T\|\leq\|K\|_2\leq1$. [given, A1, F3, F5, step 2.2]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:116:4.1 Each spatial-link transformation is a left and right translation, so it preserves its Haar factor by [F1]; the rectangle formula and uniqueness in [F6] then show the product slice measure $d\mu_s$ is preserved. Under independent gauge transformations $h,h'$ of the incoming and outgoing slices, change temporal-link variables by $\eta_x\mapsto h_x\eta_x(h'_x)^{-1}$. Each such coordinate map also preserves Haar factors by [F1], and thus preserves $d\mu_V$ by [F6]. Substitution in $Q_{x,i}=\eta_xU'_{x,i}\eta_{x+\hat i}^{-1}U_{x,i}^{-1}$ cancels the neighboring $h'$ matrices and gives $Q_{x,i}\mapsto h_xQ_{x,i}h_x^{-1}$; each spatial plaquette is likewise conjugated at its base vertex. Trace invariance therefore preserves $S_t,S_s$, and the temporal integration gives $K(h'\cdot u',h\cdot u)=K(u',u)$ for every pair $(h',h)$. In particular, setting $h=1$ gives $K(h'\cdot u',u)=K(u',u)$. The integral representation in [F5], applied almost everywhere, then gives $(T\psi)(h'\cdot u')=(T\psi)(u')$ almost everywhere for each fixed $h'$, so $T\psi\in\mathcal H_{\rm phys}$. The constant function has norm one because $d\mu_s$ is a probability and is fixed by every gauge transformation, so it is a nonzero physical vector. [given, A1, F1, F3, F5, F6, step 3.2, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:118:5.1 Each $\mathcal U(h)$ is unitary by measure preservation from step 4.1; the Hilbert structure follows from [F5] under full AC [A1]. Its fixed space is the kernel of the bounded operator $\mathcal U(h)-I$, hence is closed; the intersection over all $h$ is $\mathcal H_{\rm phys}$, so $\mathcal H_{\rm phys}$ is closed. [given, A1, F5, step 4.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined.md:120:6.1 In the cyclic product of kernels, each time slice contributes one half of $S_s(u_t)$ from the incoming factor and one half from the outgoing factor, summing to the full spatial action. Each temporal-link integral contributes exactly $e^{-S_t(u_{t+1},u_t;\eta_t)}$. Therefore the product integrand before temporal-link integration is the full $e^{-S_\Lambda}$; finite-product Tonelli [F4] gives the cyclic-kernel integral $Z_\Lambda$. For a bounded slice observable $O(u_0)$, the absolute value of its inserted integrand is bounded by $\|O\|_\infty e^{-S_\Lambda}\leq\|O\|_\infty$ on the finite product probability space [F6], so it is integrable. Applying [F4] to the positive and negative parts of its real and imaginary components justifies exchanging the integrations; the result is the corresponding unnormalized finite-volume expectation. [given, F3, F4, F6, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
